WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.215
For the exam, it is important to know what can interfere with both wired and wireless networks.

00:06.215 --> 00:14.723
For wired networks, specifically those using twisted-pair cable, data transmissions can be affected as a result of nearby power cables,

00:14.723 --> 00:17.832
electromagnetic interference, and fluorescent lighting.

00:17.832 --> 00:28.454
For wireless networks, the following can interfere with a wireless signal: heavy machinery, such as elevator shafts; walls, especially for 5 GHz networks;

00:28.454 --> 00:35.622
and anything else that runs on the 2.4 GHz frequency, including items such as microwave ovens and cordless phones.

00:35.622 --> 00:45.832
For both wired and wireless networks, be aware that there could be people trying to intercept data. Important data should be encrypted while being transmitted.

00:45.832 --> 00:52.299
This way, the data is no good to someone who hacks into the network unless that person also has the decryption key.

00:52.299 --> 01:03.546
Also, as with other computer equipment, heavy fluctuations in electricity, such as brownouts, blackouts, spikes, or surges can also cause a problem with data transmissions.

01:03.546 --> 01:13.746
Even lightning can be a problem. Twisted-pair cables are also subject to crosstalk, which occurs when a signal on one pair of wires interferes with another wire or pair of wires.

01:13.746 --> 01:18.139
This is usually a result of a damaged cable, which should be replaced.

01:18.139 --> 01:27.490
For the exam, the most important takeaway from these topics is to understand what can cause interference with data transmissions.

